Docker Commands and Practice

Overview

During my internship learning, I practiced basic Docker commands used for managing images and containers. These commands helped me understand the basic Docker workflow through the command line.

1. Docker Version

The following command is used to check the installed Docker version.

docker --version

2. Downloading an Image

The docker pull command is used to download an image from Docker Hub.

Example:

docker pull nginx

3. Listing Docker Images

The docker images command displays the Docker images available on the local system.

docker images

4. Running a Container

The docker run command is used to create and start a container from an image.

Example:

docker run nginx

5. Running a Container in Background

The `-d` option runs the container in detached mode.

Example:

docker run -d nginx

6. Checking Running Containers

The following command displays currently running containers:

docker ps

7. Checking All Containers

To display both running and stopped containers:

docker ps -a

8. Stopping a Container

The docker stop command is used to stop a running container.

Example:

docker stop <container_id>

9. Starting a Container

A stopped container can be started again using:

docker start <container_id>

10. Removing a Container

The docker rm command is used to remove a container.

Example:

docker rm <container_id>

11. Removing an Image

The docker rmi command is used to remove a Docker image.

Example:

docker rmi <image_id>

12. Viewing Container Logs

The docker logs command can be used to view the output generated by a container.

Example:

docker logs <container_id>

13. Port Mapping

Port mapping allows an application running inside a container to be accessed through a port on the host machine.

Example:

docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx

Here:

- `8080` is the port on the host machine.
- `80` is the port inside the container.

14. Basic Docker Command Summary

Command | Purpose

docker --version | Check Docker version

docker pull | Download an image

docker images | List images

docker run | Create and start a container

docker ps | List running containers

docker ps -a | List all containers

docker start | Start a stopped container

docker stop | Stop a running container

docker rm | Remove a container

docker rmi | Remove an image

docker logs | View container logs

docker build | Build an image from a Dockerfile

15. Basic Practice Workflow

A simple Docker practice workflow is:

Pull an image
      ↓
Check the image
      ↓
Run a container
      ↓
Check the container
      ↓
View logs
      ↓
Stop the container
      ↓
Start the container again
      ↓
Remove the container when no longer required

16. Learning Outcome

Through this practice, I became familiar with commonly used Docker commands and learned how to perform basic image and container management from the command line.

I also practiced running containers, checking their status, stopping and starting them, viewing logs, removing containers, and performing basic port mapping.
